PREREQUISITES
=============

C99 compliant C compiler
POSIX compliant make

Libraries and Headers required for building:

libX11 
libXt
libXext
libXrender
libXrandr
libXinerama
libXm (open-motif/motif)

BUILDING AND INSTALLING
=======================

Make sure to review the target platform makefile (mf/Makefile.<platform>).
Adjust the installation prefix and X resources installation path if necessary.
Read comments in the makefile for additional compile time options available.
The target platform is picked by the master Makefile from uname(1). If no
platform specific Makefile exists, try "Makefile.generic".

Run 'make' in the top-level directory of the source distribution.
When the build process finishes, run 'make install' as root.

CONFIGURATION
=============

The app-defaults file installed in PREFIX/X11/app-defaults/Emwm includes basic
font and color configuration for window decorations. See emwm(1) Component
Appearance Resources section for more information.

A sample emwmrc, which defines key bindings and root/window menus, is installed
in PREFIX/X11/system.emwmrc. This file may be copied to $HOME/.emwmrc for
customization. The emwmrc(4) man-page describes the syntax in detail.

IMPORTANT NOTE ON EXISTING RC FILES
-----------------------------------

Any existing EMWM 1.3 (or earlier) configuration files will interfere with the
workspace functionality, since they don't have any related bindings (window and
root menu) defined. The distribution archive includes an updated template file.

TRANSPARENCY
============

ARGB application windows retain their per-pixel alpha through the window
frame. An external X11 compositor, such as picom, is required to display
transparency. Configure background opacity in the terminal/application;
the Motif title bar, controls, borders and matte remain opaque.

EMWM does not start a compositor automatically. No compositor-specific
opacity rule is required for application-provided ARGB transparency.

KEYBOARD WINDOW TILING
======================

Super+Left/Right places the focused window in the left/right half of its
monitor. Super+Up maximizes; Super+Down restores. After selecting a half,
Up selects its upper quarter and Down selects its lower quarter. Down from
a quarter restores the original floating geometry; Up from a quarter
maximizes. Left/Right always select a half again. There is no timing limit
and Super may be released between shortcuts.

The same bindings cover KP_Left/Right/Up/Down (keypad 4/6/8/2) with Num Lock
on or off. Super is the existing EMWM Mod4 modifier. The ordinary Motif
move, resize, maximize, minimize and restore commands remain available.
A committed move/resize starts a new floating geometry. Minimizing or using
the traditional maximize/restore pair preserves the tiling restore point.

New installations use these bindings from system.emwmrc (and the built-in
fallback). Existing personal key sets are NOT changed automatically. Add
these lines inside the Keys section selected by Emwm*keyBindings in your
~/.emwmrc, then restart EMWM to use them:

    Super<Key>Left     window f.tile left
    Super<Key>Right    window f.tile right
    Super<Key>Up       window f.tile up
    Super<Key>Down     window f.tile down
    Super<Key>KP_Left  window f.tile left
    Super<Key>KP_Right window f.tile right
    Super<Key>KP_Up    window f.tile up
    Super<Key>KP_Down  window f.tile down

Remove these lines from your selected key set to disable the shortcuts.
The configurable f.tile function also accepts top-left, top-right,
bottom-left, bottom-right and restore. For example, optional direct
quadrants on the keypad (no additional defaults/conflicts imposed):

    Super Ctrl<Key>KP_Home  window f.tile top-left
    Super Ctrl<Key>KP_Prior window f.tile top-right
    Super Ctrl<Key>KP_End   window f.tile bottom-left
    Super Ctrl<Key>KP_Next  window f.tile bottom-right
    Super Ctrl<Key>Down    window f.tile restore

Regions include the Motif frame and matte. The monitor with the greatest
frame intersection is used (nearest center if entirely off-screen), via
RandR 1.5 logical monitors, falling back to existing Xinerama or the X
screen. Visible panels' _NET_WM_STRUT_PARTIAL/_NET_WM_STRUT reservations are
read on demand, including workspace and monitor ranges. Geometry changes
are picked up on the next shortcut; already tiled windows are not continually
rearranged. No polling, extra process or build dependency is introduced.

Application minimum/maximum sizes, increments and aspect ratios are honored.
A constrained window can leave a gap; an impossible region is a no-op.
Dialogs, transients, special EWMH windows, fullscreen windows and windows
that disallow movement/resizing are excluded. Restore obeys current size
hints and clamps to the current work area if the monitor layout changed.
The floating restore point is session-local, not persisted across WM restart.
Only keyboard tiling is provided; dragging to screen edges is unchanged.

Run 'make test-tiling' for XTest keyboard/geometry tests on private Xvfb
servers, including two RandR logical monitors, changing panel reservations,
Num/Caps Lock, all quarters, restore, hints, alternate frames and opt-out.
This exercises logical monitors; physical hotplug/GPU combinations still
need testing on actual multi-monitor systems.

REGRESSION TESTS
================

Run 'make test' after installing the build prerequisites plus Xvfb and the
XTest and XComposite development libraries (libxtst-dev and libxcomposite-dev
on Debian). The tests start a private X server; they do not run against or
replace the desktop window manager.

The suite exercises close-button clicks and cancellation, WM_DELETE_WINDOW
and its timestamp, the fallback for clients without that protocol, Motif
function/decorations restrictions and changes, narrow-window sizing, and
minimize/maximize/restore. It runs with recessed frames and with slab frames
using a separate title appearance.

XVFB may specify an alternate Xvfb executable. Compiler/linker flags for the
test client can be supplied via CC, CPPFLAGS, CFLAGS and LDFLAGS. To run one
configuration directly, use tests/run-x11.sh; FRAME_STYLE=slab and
DECOUPLED_TITLE=1 select the alternate appearance.

Run 'make test-argb' with picom installed to also check 0%, 50% and 100%
alpha, opaque decorations, actual composited pixels, title repainting and
fullscreen transitions. This runs the button tests with ARGB clients too,
including a slab-frame configuration with a matte and Xft title fonts.
PICOM may specify an alternate compositor executable. The runner supplies
an isolated picom configuration using its XRender backend.

ABOUT THIS FORK
===============
This fork is maintained at https://github.com/pjdgilmour/emwm .
Upstream: https://github.com/alx210/emwm (alx@fastestcode.org), based on Motif.
Local changes add a classic Motif close button and preserve ARGB transparency
with an external compositor, plus configurable keyboard window tiling.
Original copyright and license notices remain
applicable; see COPYING and individual source headers.
Debian integration: https://github.com/pjdgilmour/emwm-desktop .
